# Which AWS project this platform builds in. Owner: integrator.
#
# Read by tools/preflight.sh, which fails when the credentials in use do not match. Source it
# before an apply:
#
#   set -a; . tools/aws-project.env; set +a
#
# The keys are AWS_PROFILE and AWS_REGION, the names the AWS CLI and Terraform already read, rather
# than names of this repository's own. A SPA_-prefixed key would source cleanly, export nothing the
# apply consults, and leave a recipe that reads like a guard and is inert -- which is the shape of
# the failure this file exists to stop.
#
# Why this file exists. On 2026-10-15 a sitting applied the bootstrap root into the sample project
# that the new AWS experience creates beside the one made for this work, and nothing in the
# repository said which was intended: a prompt asking "is this the right account?" had no value to
# check against, and the answer was yes. The sitting then read the other project's guardrails as a
# platform problem for most of an evening, and this file recorded the sample project's profile as
# the intended one. On 2026-10-17 the stack was bootstrapped again into the project made for the
# work, and the sample project's state was archived beside bootstrap's. The profile name below was
# right both times, which is the lesson: `aws login` stores whichever console session the human
# picks, so a profile name does not pin a project. tools/aws-project.local.env, git-ignored and one
# line (AWS_ACCOUNT_ID=...), pins the account; tools/preflight.sh compares the session to it.
#
# A profile NAME and a Region, and deliberately no account id. Change request 1.5 kept account
# identifiers out of the tracked tree when it ignored *.tfvars and backend.hcl, on the reasoning
# that an account id is not a secret but is still an identifier in a public repository; the same
# reasoning is applied here, and it is an extension of that decision rather than something 1.5
# already settled. Be clear about what the profile buys: it pins the NAME, and the name's meaning
# lives in ~/.aws/config. preflight.sh checks that the profile in use is this one and compares
# the resolved account with the separate pin in tools/aws-project.local.env.

# The named profile in ~/.aws/config. Not "default": that is what went wrong.
AWS_PROFILE=spa-platform

# The project's selected Region. A project is provisioned in one Region and the ARNs of the
# Regional resources created in it carry that Region:
# https://docs.aws.amazon.com/accounts/latest/reference/project-regions.html
#
# All four Terraform roots default to us-east-1 -- infra/bootstrap and infra/envs/poc/{apps,idp,
# platform} -- so each needs region set explicitly in its own terraform.tfvars to match this value.
AWS_REGION=us-east-2
